You can add a new function in migrate.js like this:

```javascript
const matchIdentifiers = (sourceData, targetData) => {
    return sourceData.filter(sourceItem =>
        targetData.some(targetItem => targetItem.id === sourceItem.id)
    );
};
```
This function filters `sourceData` for items that have matching `id`s in `targetData`.

In test/migrate.test.js, you can add the following test cases:

```javascript
describe('matchIdentifiers', () => {
    it('should return matched identifiers', () => {
        const source = [{ id: 1 }, { id: 2 }, { id: 3 }];
        const target = [{ id: 2 }, { id: 3 }];
        const result = matchIdentifiers(source, target);
        expect(result).toEqual([{ id: 2 }, { id: 3 }]);
    });

    it('should return an empty array for no matches', () => {
        const source = [{ id: 1 }];
        const target = [{ id: 4 }];
        const result = matchIdentifiers(source, target);
        expect(result).toEqual([]);
    });
});
```
These cover both matching and non-matching scenarios.

This error typically indicates that one of the items in `sourceData` does not have an `id` property. You should ensure all items being processed have the required structure. You can add a check in your matchIdentifiers function:

```javascript
const matchIdentifiers = (sourceData, targetData) => {
    return sourceData.filter(sourceItem =>
        sourceItem.id !== undefined &&
        targetData.some(targetItem => targetItem.id === sourceItem.id)
    );
};
```
This will prevent undefined `id` errors.

To optimize the function, consider using a Set for target IDs to reduce lookup time:

```javascript
const matchIdentifiers = (sourceData, targetData) => {
    const targetIds = new Set(targetData.map(item => item.id));
    return sourceData.filter(sourceItem => targetIds.has(sourceItem.id));
};
```
This approach significantly improves performance, especially with larger datasets.
